WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.357 --> 00:00:02.190
SPEAKER: When it comes
to cost optimization,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.523
you want to make sure that
you're running your workloads

00:00:04.523 --> 00:00:05.820
as efficiently as possible.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.610
Of course, that definitely
means not paying for resources

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.810
that you're not using.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:13.170
Today, we'll talk about Google
Kubernetes Engine's autoscaling

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.120
and go over some tips.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.740
Let's go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.135
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.700
One major advantage
of Kubernetes

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.890
is how it was built
with scaling in mind.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.045
And GKE adds even
more options to scale.

00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:33.170
It can work pretty well
without much configuration,

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.180
but there's a lot
of cost optimization

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.380
potential by going deeper.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.320
In the last video,
we talked a lot

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.870
about how to choose
the right machine type.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.630
As always, it really depends
on knowing your application's

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:45.980
resource needs.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.280
And that's just as important
for configuring autoscaling.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.040
I'll start with an introduction
to autoscaling, and then dive

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.710
deep into horizontal
and vertical autoscaling

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.330
for your workloads.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.520
Starting with the basics,
autoscaling is really

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.410
all about two things--

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.740
getting additional resources
when demand increases

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.870
and shutting down or
removing resources

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.760
when demand decreases.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.560
By setting autoscaling
up efficiently

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.480
and specific to your
workload, you'll

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.670
be able to minimize
waste and cost.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.460
In order to understand how you
should configure autoscaling,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.390
let's simplify everything into
workloads and infrastructure.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.580
Workloads are made up of
pods, which are running

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.770
containerized applications.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.850
Infrastructure is
made up of nodes,

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.820
which are responsible
for the actual resources,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.250
like computing
capacity and memory.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.770
For each of these, there's
also two ways to scale--

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:35.870
horizontally or vertically.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.068
Horizontal scaling is done
by adding more pods or nodes.

00:01:39.068 --> 00:01:40.610
If there's a lot of
demand coming in,

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.950
the autoscaler can try to
increase the number of pods

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.930
or nodes to handle it.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.440
Vertical scaling is done by
making pods or nodes bigger.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.290
When demand increases,
a larger pod or node

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.320
may be able to reduce the
burden by handling more.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.030
With those basics,
let's talk about how

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.400
GKE handles autoscaling.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.410
Let's start with the
workload and pod size

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.560
by looking at the aptly named
horizontal pod autoscaler

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.850
and vertical pod autoscaler.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.430
For horizontal
scaling, you'll need

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.949
a metric that represents
the demand on your workload,

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:13.790
such as CPU utilization
or requests per second.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.400
With that target, the
horizontal pod autoscaler

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.520
can try to add pods to get your
workload back to your desired

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.630
state.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.620
Adding pods should mean that
the work is spread out evenly,

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.150
and a metric like
average CPU utilization

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.670
would go down across your pods.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.590
Similarly, if there's
not much demand,

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.510
the autoscaler can
remove pods, which

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.850
is great for saving on costs.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.183
When using a horizontal
pod autoscaler,

00:02:37.183 --> 00:02:38.600
remember that there
might be a lag

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.683
time depending on how long
it takes your container

00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:41.900
to start up.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.540
Setting the correct amount
of available metric space,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.720
or buffer, requires knowing
how well you can handle spikes

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:48.770
in demand.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.560
If the buffer is too small
and the pods take a long time

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.320
to spin up, your app
might get overloaded.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.120
On the other hand,
too large of a buffer

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.520
can lead to wasted
resources and extra costs.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.730
So a few things to keep in mind.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.490
Understand what capacity
might be needed for a spike,

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.870
and set your buffer accordingly.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.570
Do what you can to have your
pods spin up quickly and shut

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.450
down efficiently.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.220
Make sure to set up
the right readiness

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.948
and live probes so GKE can
make the best decisions.

00:03:15.948 --> 00:03:17.990
And remember to make sure
that your metric server

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:19.518
is always up and running.

00:03:19.518 --> 00:03:21.560
I know we haven't talked
about what some of these

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.720
mean, but keep them
in mind, and we'll

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.400
come back to these
when we dive deeper

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.490
into optimizing
your applications.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.120
So that's horizontal
pod autoscaling.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.840
Now, let's look at
vertical pod autoscaling.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.950
Rather than adding
additional pod replicas,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.650
here, the pods
actually get recreated

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.990
with more or less
resources, depending

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.000
on CPU and memory usage.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.877
Remember that too few resources
can overload your apps

00:03:45.877 --> 00:03:48.460
and too many resources will mean
that you're paying for things

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.080
that you're not using.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.330
Vertical pod autoscaling
works to solve this.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:54.100
It has three modes--

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.900
off, initial, and auto.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.990
When it's off, no
autoscaling actually happens,

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.450
but the autoscaler is
looking at your pods

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.520
and creating recommendations
based on usage

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.510
that you can inspect, which also
happens in the other two modes.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.970
If it's set to initial,
those recommendations

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.630
will be used to create resized
pods as they're needed.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:17.420
After creating a pod at
the recommended size,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.660
based on those recommendations,
it won't change the pod size

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.040
afterward.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.050
If the autoscaler
is set to auto,

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.200
those recommendations will be
used to regularly resize pods

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.250
by deleting them and recreating
them with the adjusted amounts.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.920
In general, you should make sure
that vertical pod autoscaling

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.830
is off for at least a
day, or ideally a week,

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.390
in order to collect
recommendation data.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:42.800
Then, you can switch to initial
or auto based on your needs,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.410
or you can just keep it off
and review the recommendations

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.660
to manually adjust your pods.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.670
Here's some things
to keep in mind.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:51.860
If you're looking to
handle sudden spikes,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.380
use horizontal pod
autoscaling instead.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.450
Vertical pod autoscaling
can take a bit longer

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:59.150
than horizontal since it
can grow the number of pods

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.480
without taking any offline.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.640
Make sure that your app
can actually benefit

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.410
from additional resources.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:06.910
If it's single-threaded
or limited

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.100
by workers, rather
than resources,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.010
vertical pod autoscaling
might not help at all.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:15.910
Set minimums and maximums
and avoid any large changes,

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.300
since abrupt changes can
require new deployments.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.090
Don't forget to keep your
metric server healthy.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:23.650
Note auto-provisioning,
which we'll

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.330
talk more about
in the next video,

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:27.610
can help by creating new
nodes that can better

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.710
fit your resized pods.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.380
And if you're using auto,
make sure that your workload

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.360
can handle restarts.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.310
Also, you'll want to
choose a pod disruption

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.330
budget to control the number
of changes happening at once.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.960
You can use both horizontal
and vertical pod autoscaling

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.120
at the same time, but
there's a few things

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.860
that you need to
be careful about.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.650
First of all, you shouldn't
use them on the same resource,

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.450
like CPU or memory.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.600
Both autoscalers are trying
to react to changes in demand

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.070
on that same metric,
and that could

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.170
cause overlapping problems.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.510
But you can always keep
vertical pod autoscaler

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.670
in off mode to get
recommendations

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.950
without any changes
actually being made,

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.560
along with using
horizontal pod autoscaling,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.390
so your app can
scale horizontally

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:12.480
based on CPU utilization
while you can make decisions

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.930
based on those vertical pod
autoscaling recommendations.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.770
Or, you can use a custom metric
for the horizontal autoscaler,

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.303
like requests per second.

00:06:20.303 --> 00:06:22.470
In addition, you should
ensure that your deployments

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.360
are receiving
enough traffic when

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.280
both autoscalers are enabled.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.260
That way, the vertical
part autoscaler

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.890
can get accurate data for
creating recommendations.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.260
As always, it depends
on your workload

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.930
to know whether you should
use horizontal, vertical,

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:38.580
or both pod autoscalers.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.380
But we're not stopping there.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.680
On the next video, we'll cover
the cluster autoscaler and node

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:44.698
auto-provisioning.

00:06:44.698 --> 00:06:46.740
Remember to check out the
link in the description

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.500
for the full guide on
GKE cost optimization.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.150
